\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{How we synthesize a task interface}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}

\p{We synthesize the task and task-law pair introduced by the preceding task
interface as a common interface for response tasks and interactive
tasks. Benchmark datasets usually present an input and expect an answer in a
parseable format. Reinforcement-learning formulations instead name states,
actions, and reward; see \citet{Chapter 3}{sutton2018reinforcement}. The
admissibility predicate captures parseability, while the law records how
instances are sampled. Neither choice already identifies parsing with success.}

\p{Later definitions attach either a one-shot inference protocol or an
interactive environment to this interface. Theorems can then state which
properties require only a task law and which require the additional temporal
structure of an environment.}
